{
  "_comment": "EXAMPLE ONLY. The real file lives at ~/.claude/mission-control.json and is written by `/mc deploy` the first time you run it on a machine. Do not copy this into a repo — spawn preferences are per-person, and a clone carrying someone else's terminal choice is a bug, not a default.",

  "spawn": {
    "mode": "background",
    "date": "2026-08-24",
    "launchCommand": "claude",
    "permissionMode": null
  },

  "naming": {
    "_comment": "USUALLY ABSENT, and absent is the correct default. Nothing here is asked for: a session names itself and says what it took. Write a key here only when the user STATES how they want their fleets named -- never because they used a name once while discussing one project. That is an example, and it belongs in that project's own MISSION-CONTROL.md. A call-sign is what people say; a handle is what peers address, and a handle is NEVER derived from a call-sign -- for a call-sign the user typed that cannot be a session name, ask.",
    "_exampleIsNotAPreference": "Measured 2026-08-18 and corrected 2026-08-22. 'FLEET COMMAND' was said about one repo, recorded here as the machine's coordinator, and every other fleet with no coordinator on its board then resolved to it. When you cannot tell a preference from an example, it is an example: an unrecorded preference costs one moment of deriving CONTROL, a recorded example renames the coordinator of every fleet the user owns.",
    "coordinator": "CONTROL",
    "stationStyle": "same",
    "handles": {
      "FLEET COMMAND": "FLEETCOM"
    }
  },

  "_fields": {
    "mode": "background | window | tab. SUPERSEDED BY env.MC_SPAWN_MODE in ~/.claude/settings.json, which is where the user changes it now. Otherwise the only key that matters, and it is the answer to a question the user was ASKED on their first deploy -- never detected. Absent means nobody has been asked yet, which is deliberately NOT the same as background: they behave alike and mean opposite things, and collapsing them is how the ask never happens. background = `claude --bg`, no terminal at all, works identically on every OS and in every IDE and CLI; measured 2026-08-23 to register in the fleet manifest and answer a radio check by call-sign. window = a visible window opened through the terminal's OWN published API (iTerm2 `create tab`, Terminal.app `do script`, tmux new-window, kitty, WezTerm, Windows Terminal) -- never a synthesised keystroke. Set it by hand with spawn-pref.sh set <mode>, or clear it with spawn-pref.sh reset to be asked again.",
    "date": "when the answer was recorded. Written by spawn-pref.sh; nothing reads it back.",
    "launchCommand": "usually `claude`; override if the CLI is installed under another name",
    "coordinator": "OPTIONAL, and normally omitted. What THIS user calls the coordinating station ON EVERY FLEET -- CONTROL, FLEET COMMAND, or their own word. The skill has no opinion and must not act as though it does. Omitted means: this project's MISSION-CONTROL.md decides, else plain CONTROL. Do not write it to record a name you merely saw used.",
    "stationStyle": "same | long-callsign-short-handle | per-station -- how this user wants the OTHER stations named. \"same\" is one word for both. \"long-callsign-short-handle\" allows phrases on the radio with short addresses (CHANNELS & INTEGRATIONS -> CHANNELS). \"per-station\" means ask each time a call-sign is initiated.",
    "handles": "call-sign -> address, only for the ones that differ. A call-sign with a space cannot be a session name; a user may also just prefer a short address for a long call-sign. Both are legitimate and neither is derivable, so both are asked.",
    "permissionMode": "null by default, and deploy NEVER sets this on your behalf. null means a spawned station prompts for its own pushes, exactly like any session. A BACKGROUND station has no window for that prompt to appear in, so deploy's report hands you `claude attach <id>` -- that is the one real cost of not opening a terminal, and it is surfaced rather than papered over. Setting this to anything else widens what a spawned session may do unattended — your choice, in your own file."
  },

  "_hostsVerified": {
    "background (any host)": "VERIFIED END TO END 2026-08-23 on Claude Code 2.1.241: `claude --bg --name X` in an untrusted worktree returned in ~1.5s, registered in the fleet manifest as `bg · idle`, and ANSWERED a radio check sent to it by call-sign. No terminal, no keystrokes, no focus change. This is the default and needs no host support of any kind.",
    "print (any host)": "VERIFIED end-to-end 2026-08-18. No permissions, no timing, nothing to mistype.",
    "Apple_Terminal (window)": "`do script` is Terminal's own API and opens a WINDOW -- Terminal.app publishes no scriptable new-tab. Needs the Automation grant only. THE TAB PATH IS GONE: it required System Events to press Cmd-T, and it failed in the field twice. 2026-08-17, a lost modifier race corrupted the command into `tcd`. 2026-08-23, deploying three stations, three tabs came up EMPTY and all three launch commands were typed into Control's own prompt, interleaved and corrupted (`ntialcd '/Users/...'`). Targeting by tty and verifying afterwards did not remove that race, it only made it observable -- so the requirement for a tab was removed instead.",
    "tmux": "`new-window` -- the portable visible recipe: macOS, Linux, WSL, and inside IDE terminals.",
    "iTerm.app / kitty / WezTerm / WindowsTerminal / gnome-terminal / konsole / xfce4-terminal / alacritty / ghostty / xterm": "recipes shipped, UNVERIFIED. Each says so when it uses one.",
    "vscode / cursor / windsurf / jetbrains": "no window recipe exists and none will be invented -- nothing outside the editor can open its integrated terminal reliably. `--window` declines and says why; background mode needs no host, so these deploy normally."
  }
}
